<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation by default. Test projects opt out by
    setting <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    in their csproj. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because
    the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was previously gated on
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild, which made a guarantee this file states depend
    on a property set by a workflow in another directory — and three of the
    four members that carry this file have no workflow at all, so what reads
    here as enforcement was documentation there. A file whose promise needs an
    external switch to hold cannot state its own promise, and removing the
    condition is what makes the promise this file's own.

    The failure it prevents is not hypothetical. A committed lock file
    elsewhere in this project recorded a dependency at preview1 against a
    preview2 producer for weeks, through four arcs, and every local build
    passed: a local restore *updates* a lock rather than enforcing it, so the
    drift was invisible to every gate. Locked mode is what turns that into a
    failed restore.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now needs a restore that is
    told to re-evaluate the lock — `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`,
    which is what the CLI's force-evaluate switch sets — rather than a plain
    restore. One command, once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole
    purpose is that it does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as
    a property because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
